<BlogPost {...meta}>
  <p>Nearly every loan officer has, at some point, walked a box of donuts into a real estate office and hoped for the best. It almost never works, and after 25 years in this business I think I finally understand why. Figuring out how to get Realtor referrals as a loan officer is only half the job, and it is the easier half. The half that decides your income is whether the agent is still sending you buyers two years later.</p>

  <p>Most advice on this topic stops at the first half: prospect harder, show up at open houses, co-host a first-time buyer seminar. Some of that helps. But I have watched plenty of LOs win an agent's first referral and quietly lose the fifth, and the reasons had nothing to do with rate or product. So this is the playbook for both halves: earning the referral, and then being the partner an agent has no reason to replace.</p>

  <h2>What an agent is actually buying when they refer you</h2>

  <p>Start with the agent's side of it. When an agent hands you a buyer, they are not doing you a favor. They are placing a bet with their own reputation. If you are slow, the buyer blames the agent for the recommendation. If the loan wobbles in escrow, the agent is the one on the phone with a nervous seller's agent. If the buyer goes quiet for six months and then buys with someone else, the agent loses the commission along with you.</p>

  <p>That bet matters more than it used to, because buyers do not shop around much once they are working with someone.</p>

  <div class="stat">
    <div class="num">74%</div>
    <div class="txt">of homebuyers interview only one agent before deciding who to work with.</div>
    <div class="src">— NAR, 2025 Profile of Home Buyers and Sellers</div>
  </div>

  <p>Agents know their business lives and dies on that first impression and on referrals from past clients. So what they want from a lender is not a lunch. They want to know that the buyer they send you will be handled well enough that it reflects well on them. Everything below comes from that one idea.</p>

  <h2>How to get Realtor referrals as a loan officer: be useful before you ask</h2>

  <h3>1. Pick a few agents, not a whole office</h3>
  <p>Working a list of 200 agents means you are a stranger to all of them. I get better results from choosing a handful of agents whose business I understand: the price points they work, the neighborhoods, whether their buyers skew first-time, VA, or move-up. Here in Honolulu that also means knowing which agents live in condo inventory and which ones spend their weekends on leasehold questions. When you know an agent's buyers, you can actually be useful to them, and you stop sounding like every other lender in their inbox.</p>

  <h3>2. Solve a problem they already have</h3>
  <p>The strongest openings I have seen come from a bad moment: a buyer whose lender has gone silent the day before an offer deadline, and an agent looking for anyone who will pick up on a Saturday. The LO who gets a letter out that afternoon tends to get the next referral too. You cannot schedule that moment, but you can make yourself the lender agents think of when something is on fire: answer the phone, give a straight read on a borderline file, and tell them the truth when a deal will not work instead of letting it drift.</p>

  <h3>3. Make pre-approvals fast and clear</h3>
  <p>Speed on letters is the most visible thing a lender does in an agent's world. A buyer who cannot get an updated pre-approval before an offer deadline makes the agent look unprepared in front of the listing side. Be the LO who turns them around the same day, and be precise about what the letter actually says. Agents and listing agents read a pre-qualification, a pre-approval, and a conditional approval very differently, and I broke that down in <a href="/blog/pre-approval-vs-prequalification">pre-approval vs. pre-qualification vs. conditional approval</a>. An agent who knows exactly what your letter means will trust it in a multiple-offer situation.</p>

  <h3>4. Take the buyers other lenders drop</h3>
  <p>Every agent has a list of buyers who are not ready: saving for a down payment, waiting out a lease, working on credit. Most lenders treat those buyers as a waste of time. Offer to take them seriously. Give each one a plan and a date, and keep the agent posted as they get closer. It is slower money, but it is the fastest way I know to show an agent that you care about their pipeline and not just this month's closing. If you want the actual language for those conversations, <a href="/blog/what-to-say-not-ready-buyer">what to say to a buyer who is not ready yet</a> has scripts for the common cases.</p>

  <h3>5. Stay on the right side of RESPA</h3>
  <p>One caution before the retention half. RESPA Section 8 prohibits giving or accepting anything of value in exchange for referrals of settlement service business. Paying for leads, covering an agent's marketing costs out of proportion to what you get, or any arrangement that looks like a referral fee in costume is a real legal exposure, for both of you. Co-marketing can be legitimate, but the costs have to be split in line with actual use, and your compliance department should see it first. Earn the referral with service. It is also the only version that lasts.</p>

  <h2>Why agents quietly stop referring</h2>

  <p>Now the half nobody writes about. In my experience, an agent almost never fires a lender after one bad closing. What happens is slower. The first buyer they sent you went quiet and the agent never heard what came of it. The second one closed, but the agent had to text you four times for status along the way. On the third, the buyer told the agent they were "approved" and the agent later found out it was a pre-qualification. None of these is a disaster. Each one is a small cost the agent paid to protect their own client.</p>

  <p>I covered where those costs come from in <a href="/blog/lo-realtor-relationship-handoff">why the LO–Realtor relationship breaks down at the handoff</a>. The short version: once the agent hands you a buyer, they lose all visibility, and every fact they need has to be requested by text. That is extra work you are creating for your best source of business, and they notice it even when they never mention it.</p>

  <p>The volume of buyers who fall into that silent stretch is large.</p>

  <div class="stat">
    <div class="num">80%</div>
    <div class="txt">of mortgage leads require nurturing before they're ready to make a decision.</div>
    <div class="src">— Mortgage Bankers Association</div>
  </div>

  <p>Most of what an agent sends you will not be closing this month. If those buyers disappear into your CRM, the agent's experience of referring to you is mostly silence. That is the pattern I described in <a href="/blog/the-follow-up-gap">The Follow-Up Gap</a>, and it is the one that costs partnerships.</p>

  <h2>How to keep a Realtor referral partner: make them look good</h2>

  <p>The test I use now is simple. After every interaction, did this agent look more organized to their client, or less? A few habits move that answer in the right direction.</p>

  <h3>Confirm every referral fast</h3>
  <p>When an agent sends a name, they should know within the day that you reached out and what happened. Even "left a voicemail, will try again tomorrow" is better than nothing. The agent's biggest fear is that their client was never called.</p>

  <h3>Make status something they can see, not something they chase</h3>
  <p>An agent should never have to text you to learn what stage a file is in. If your status only exists in your head and your LOS, every question costs you both time and costs the agent a little confidence. Put it somewhere they can look.</p>

  <h3>Tell the truth on the slow ones</h3>
  <p>For buyers who are months out, send the agent the reason and the date: "Saving for another four months, I'll check in with her in January." That one sentence tells the agent you are working their client and gives them something to say if the buyer calls them first. The <a href="/blog/5-touch-follow-up-cadence">5-touch follow-up cadence</a> covers how to keep those buyers warm in the meantime.</p>

  <h3>Keep one version of the story</h3>
  <p>Agree on vocabulary with each partner. When you say conditional approval and they say approved, the buyer hears two different things. Use the same terms for the same stages, and the borrower stops getting mixed signals from the two people they trust most in the transaction.</p>

  <h3>Close the loop after closing</h3>
  <p>A short note to the agent after funding, with a thank-you and a heads-up about anything that might matter to the client later, costs five minutes. It is also the moment the agent decides whether to send you the next one.</p>

  <h2>Where DealSync fits</h2>

  <p>I built DealSync because I kept failing at the retention half, not from lack of effort but because the work had nowhere to live. My pipeline was in one system and the agent's clients were in another, so every update depended on me remembering to send it.</p>

  <p>DealSync is a shared deal room between the loan officer and the agent. Each buyer gets one deal card that both sides can see. When I take a referral, the agent sees that the buyer was contacted, what stage they are in, and why a not-ready buyer is not ready yet along with the date I plan to work them again. The borrower can get a live status link of their own, so the "any update?" texts slow down on all three sides.</p>

  <p>Agents get notified only on the milestones they care about: pre-approval, conditional approval, appraisal received, clear to close, funded, and when a letter they asked for is ready. They can request a pre-approval letter from the card, and it goes out on my letterhead in about a minute. On my side, the AI Coach reads the deal notes and drafts the next follow-up, so the slow buyers get worked instead of forgotten.</p>

  <p>None of that replaces the relationship. It removes the small costs that wear one down, which is most of what keeping a partner comes down to.</p>

  <h2>The bottom line</h2>

  <p>Getting a Realtor's first referral takes being useful at the right moment. Keeping them takes something less glamorous: making sure that every buyer they send you makes them look a little better to their own client. Do that consistently, and you stop needing the donuts.</p>

  <p>— Jay Miller</p>
